{
  "case_id": "2001-01-679",
  "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument now in Number 01-679, Gonzaga University and Roberta S. League v. John Doe."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners (John G. Roberts, Jr.)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In 1974, when it enacted the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act, Congress conditioned Federal funding for educational institutions on the institution not having a policy or practice of releasing student records without consent. Congress did not phrase this condition in terms of individual rights. It did not, for example, follow the model of title IX, enacted 2 years earlier and also dealing with educational institutions, and say something like, no student at a school receiving Federal funds shall have his records released without his consent. Instead, Congress proceeded more indirectly. It said that no funds shall be made available to any institution having a policy or practice of releasing student records without consent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Mr. Roberts, isn't it primarily a distinction that makes a difference in connection with whether there's an implied cause of action, rather than whether 1983 authorized a cause of action?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners (John G. Roberts, Jr.)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "FERPA places a premium on voluntary compliance, on informal and inexpensive adjudication. A 1983 damages action in Federal court doesn't."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But how do you get a stop order? One of the points that were made is that if records are about to be divulged to, say, a newspaper, and the student or the parent wants an immediate stop order, you can go into court and get a TRO. There's nothing comparable in the Secretary's arsenal that has that kind of muscle behind it, is there?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0000-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They handle over 900 pieces of correspondence a year, up to -- close to 100 of things that are formally categorized as complaints as they go through the investigation stages."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, as we said in our brief, we think that the problem here is not that it's vague or amorphous, and it's not that there's not binding obligations, which is the second two prongs of the Blessing test, but the first prong of the Blessing test, while phrased in terms of benefiting individuals, the Court made clear in Blessing it's not just a general inquiry if it's of some good to people, because all legislation is of some good to somebody. It is whether it creates individual entitlements, and that's where we think this statute fails, a statute that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, with reference to the other parts, the non-(b) parts of the statute, it does seem to me that it talks about the student and the parent in very specific terms, and it uses the term, rights."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: May I ask you -- it's really the same question I asked Mr. Roberts, I suppose, but the first sentence of 1232g(a)(1)(A) refers to a policy of denying access, denying the right to inspect and review education records. In your view, is that right a federally created right, or is that a right created by some other source and, if so, what is the source of the right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Millett",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, there's a -- there is -- there's no doubt that there's a Federal level of protection here for privacy, and it's at an aggregated, collective system-wide level. It's not at the individual level of --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Brinkmann",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In FERPA, Congress gave parents the right to prevent the release of certain educational records. That's evident from Congress' choice of words and the structure and history of the statute. There are at least five indications of that intent, including references to rights under that provision, which I'll get to in a moment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When you look at this history, Congress clearly was addressing the interest of parents in controlling dissemination of information about their children. This is a paradigm example of what they were worrying about, information that's gossip, unsubstantiated, never had a chance to respond to it, could have a devastating effect on a student's career."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "ACLU",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0002-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Actually, the amicus brief of the ACLU cites a letter from the Department of Education explaining that the vast majority of private schools, elementary and secondary, do not receive Federal funding, but if I may, I think that the important point here is, the relationship of a student with a private school is different."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So what good does that do you? You have to go in --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: They're answerable to us, presumably."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-679",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e47bc430ca187ab7b369599c409808fc89e01ad2defeb605b196664cb75a80fe",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e47bc430ca18-0003-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
